1. Strategies of the Automatic Players
GeniusPlayer:
The strategy of this player is to fill the board in a sequential manner, starting from the last position where
it placed its mark. It attempts to place its mark in rows and columns, moving from left to right and top to
 bottom. If it reaches the end of a row, it moves to the next row.
CleverPlayer:
This player uses a random strategy but with an element of smartness. It tries to place its mark in a random
positionon the board, and if that position is already occupied, it chooses another random position until
it finds an empty spot. There is also a mechanism to prevent it from getting stuck in the same area.
WhateverPlayer:
This player simply chooses a random position on the board to place its mark. If that position is occupied,
it randomly selects again until it finds an empty spot. It does not have any strategy beyond this.
2. Advantages of Using a Common Interface
The advantage of designing the software so that each player class implements a common interface (Player)
is that the interface allows for polymorphism. This means that you can treat all players as if they are
of type Player,regardless of their specific classes.
OOP Pillars:
Polymorphism: It allows calling the same function on different objects without knowing their exact type.
Encapsulation: The interface provides a clear way to interact with players without exposing the internal
details of each class.
